3.4 Checkpoint
You should now have:

one row per article;
a unique article_id;
a URL for each article; and
an R data frame ready to be stored in SQLite.

6.5 Checkpoint
You should now have an R data frame containing:

the original article information;
article_id;
url; and
article_text.

8.1 Goal
Use R to transform the article text into a format that can be analyzed.
Depending on the analysis, this may include:

converting text to lowercase;
removing punctuation;
tokenizing text;
removing stop words; and
creating word frequencies.

10.1 Goal
Use R to conduct the main text analysis.
Possible analyses include:

word frequencies;
lexicon analysis;
sentiment analysis;
bigrams or n-grams;
comparisons between sources;
comparisons across time; and
other text-mining methods relevant to the research question.

11.1 Goal
Create a polished Quarto document presenting the results of the text analysis.
This working guide documents how the analysis was completed.
The final Quarto report communicates what the analysis found.
The final report may include:

research question;
data sources;
methodology;
description of the articles;
text-processing methods;
text-analysis results;
visualizations;
interpretation;
limitations; and
conclusions.

How This Guide Works
The guide combines two types of resources:

Implementation chapters introduce SQL, SQLite, databases, and the R packages used to work with them.
Project chapters provide step-by-step working plans for applying those skills to actual internship projects.

Project chapters are intentionally designed as working documents. They include:

project goals;
tasks and checkpoints;
space for working code;
space for notes and decisions;
SQL skills to practice; and
reusable workflows.

Students should add their own code and notes as they work through each project.
The goal is for these chapters to become more useful over time. After completing a project, a student should be able to return to the completed chapter as a reference when beginning a similar project in the future.

R, SQL, and SQLite
Throughout this guide, R remains the primary working environment.
We will use:

R for data preparation, analysis, visualization, and reporting;
SQL for querying, filtering, summarizing, joining, and managing relational data;
SQLite for storing project data in relational databases;
DBI and RSQLite to connect R to SQLite; and
Quarto for documenting reproducible workflows and communicating results.

The goal is not to replace R with SQL.
Instead, students will learn how SQL can become another tool within an R-based analytical workflow.

